{
  "id": "Compound::DB04843 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Mepenzolate, a cholinergic antagonist, is linked to Chrm3 through multiple pathways that converge on its role in modulating muscarinic receptor activity. The evidence suggests that Mepenzolate, as part of the cholinergic antagonist class, binds to and influences Chrm3, a muscarinic receptor involved in various physiological processes. This is supported by the fact that both Mepenzolate and Clidinium, which are also cholinergic antagonists, bind to Chrm3, indicating a shared mechanism of action. The presence of Dicyclomine, which is caused by Mepenzolate and binds to Chrm3, further reinforces the connection between Mepenzolate and Chrm3 through a causal pathway. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of Mepenzolate in activating or inhibiting muscarinic receptor signaling, potentially through the modulation of cholinergic neurotransmission. The evidence suggests that Mepenzolate exerts its effects on Chrm3 via a mechanism involving cholinergic antagonism, with the lowest common ancestor of Mepenzolate and its target compounds being an organic molecule that also acts as an antimuscarinic agent.",
